{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UEEzM5sDzz7XCqTTgsvT5i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The APC 300 run is crashing before it gets to the Round0 logging. The panic is at the GPU free line. This means a previous CUDA operation is causing an error. Let me check if there's a compilation error or an issue in the build code vs the existing code.\n\nWait — APC 0 just worked fine! The issue only appears with APC 300. But the log shows \"306 large AIRs, 0 small AIRs\" for APC 300 when it gets to Round0 at all. So the crash must be happening somewhere else.\n\n\nLooking at the backtrace, I see `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` in the trace at d_buffer.rs:178 where the GPU free fails. Since the baseline run completed successfully before my changes, something I modified must have broken APC 300 specifically. I need to trace through what I changed — the imports in mod.rs and something else — to find what's affecting the code path even when no AIRs use the batched path.\n\nThe key issue is that I switched from multi-threaded processing with 8 threads to sequential processing on a single thread. For APC 300's 306 AIRs that all classify as large, this means running them one after another instead of in parallel, which could be causing memory pressure or GPU resource issues that weren't present before. single-threaded is actually less memory-intensive.\n\nBut actually, the crash is happening before we even see the Round0 log. Let me check what's different...\n\nActually, maybe the crash IS from the Round0 code because the log output might be buffered. Let me try with explicit flushing or run with CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1.\n\nWait, I already tried that. Let me look more carefully. With the debug logging, APC 300 crashes but APC 0 works. And APC 0 output shows \"Round0: 20 large AIRs, 0 small AIRs\". So APC 0 completes Round0 successfully.\n\nFor APC 300, all 306 AIRs are classified as large, so no batching happens. The only difference from the original code is:\n1. The per-AIR processing is now sequential (not multi-threaded)\n2. The logup rules might be getting built\n\nFor large AIRs I'm building work items and processing them sequentially through the same function as before, storing the results in the batch polynomial array. This path should be identical to the original Phase 3 scatter code, just without the 8-thread parallelization. Let me restore the multi-threaded approach to see if that's where the issue lies. the temp buffers aren't freed until the async operations complete, which means sequential processing could accumulate memory across multiple AIRs. But actually, the buffers should be released when they go out of scope at the end of each `process_air_round0` call, so let me try re-enabling multi-threading for large AIRs to see if that resolves the crash.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4475,"cache_read_input_tokens":180883,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4475},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3eab5b9b-40e0-4e43-bd71-fd448feabe6e","uuid":"1d198eac-83a3-43bd-be66-8fc9df106788"}
